Project #2 - Developing Projects Website (Primpt and Feedback forum) - Project 2 - Feedback Forum
This is a sort of continuation from Project # 1. Build a multipage web site using HTML/CSS, media, and links using the material from Project # 1 and more.  You'll use this as your project demo website for other work in this class. 

Key Elements Needed
A welcome or "splash" page, where visitors get first impressions of your work ("index" page). 
A 'stylepage.css' used for coding style into the entire site (no 'inline' .css on this site) Little to no .JS code.
An About US page, taking from info from Project # 1 and making this into a web page. 

A resource page for implicit bias, in the style of a 'Library Resource Guide pageLinks to an external site.'. This should include some ORIGINAL thoughts on the IBT, intersectionality, and implicit bias, with links and resources for people encountering these terms for the first time (Audience: Think first-year college students.) 
 
A 'tech heroes' page.  A short bio and links to someone who is challenging norms within the tech space. It should be a person of color or ally who is providing leadership in advancing equity for race/class/gender in the United States.

No CEOs from Google or Microsoft here; We've all heard of Ada Lovelace, Grace Hopper, and the other "Hidden Figures." — Find someone you know personally, or stretch yourself and profile someone who others are unlikely to know because they're not in the news everyday. Need some ideas? Go to peopleofcolorintech.comLinks to an external site.. (300 word max profile with useful links).
Evaluation 

Design Points

Complete Module 2 and Module 3 readings and exercises, and you should be well on your way to developing a site using HTML. This site must be 'hand-coded' HTML (no templates from WordPress. If there is use of templates, these must be heavily modified and commented on). 

Module 4's resources will help with design, do not to lean too hard on 'Material' and other basic resources - This is will impact your grade. 

Code Points

You must GitHub to upload your site and store your code, so you can share your code with peers by other means. See 'Obtain a Web site'
Every page must have 'html/CSS comments' (see Module 2 videos). 
Each page must contain at least 100 words of commenting explaining your coding choices.
Each person must initial their name in the comments like so <!-- ahsdfh sahdjf   RR --> 
Zero points will be awarded for sites built using generative AI. Gen-AI can be useful for finding key features to use. You must insert your own code into your designs.   
 
